## Your Task

1. Fill in the `defender_vulnerabilities` field — one entry per defender belief PM ID, describing what makes each process model part exploitable in the context of this ICA.

2. Generate the `attacker_bdi` — beliefs (what the attacker knows about defender weaknesses), desires (which ICA to induce), and intentions (how to manipulate the control loop, referencing specific PM-*, FB-*, or CA-* elements).

3. Return a non-null `unsafe_outcome` condition compatible with the selected
UCA type. Use a placeholder object with exactly `binding_ref`, `value_type`,
`description`, `minimum`, and `maximum` for an unknown scalar and derive
`semantic_binding_required` from placeholder presence; do not invent runtime
fields, tools, endpoints, prompts, clocks, or detector behavior.

The placeholder shape is:

```json
{
  "binding_ref": "SEM-1",
  "value_type": "integer",
  "description": "Maximum supported delay in this deployment.",
  "minimum": 0,
  "maximum": null
}
```

Use only `integer`, `number`, `string`, or `boolean` value types. Allowed
condition variants are NOT_PROVIDED=`action_presence`, INCORRECT=`action_value`
or `state_value`, WRONG_TIMING=`ordering`/`delay`/`window`/`absence`, and
WRONG_DURATION=`duration`. A typed unsafe condition may repeat the exact
selected CA/CM action reference; causal-factor sources remain the exact
structural PM/FB/CA identities from the selected path. Only delay/duration/
window numeric fields may contain numeric placeholders; ordering and absence
use exact step/reference identities.

Return the result as a JSON object matching the structure defined in the system prompt.
